content.thinking:
Very revealing:
- mountedCount = 10 total object nodes but only 1 is a flow object, and it's at x=344 (left side). So the camera is NOT centered on the flow objects!
- Statusbar "TRACE · architecture" (zoom 0.5 ✓).
- fitView({nodes: targetNodes}) ran... 80ms after selection. But then my mount camera effect? No — fittedLayoutRef prevents refit. Hmm wait — ANOTHER effect could have moved the camera: the dock shift effect! Sequence: select flow → mode becomes trace → corridorDocked false→true → dock effect shifts y by -70 ✓ (small). But camera at translate(-1163, -1593) — where is that? The default camera was (-2328, -2163) at 0.85 → at 0.5, center of map ≈ viewport/2 - 0.5×center... 

Wait, actually let me think about what fitView(nodes=flowObjects, padding .12, minZoom .5) produced. The flow bbox for objects 0..7: they're in regions 001-006 across 2 rows of the System region. The composition: complex system 7295×5903 total. Rows of regions: row1 = regions {001,002,003}, row2 = {004,005,006}... wait nested: complex regionCount 9 → system children 001-006, nested 007,008 under others. Objects 0..7 → regionIds = leaf regions [001..006] (6 leafs) → objects 0,6 in 001; 1,7 in 002; 2 in 003; 3 in 004; 4 in 005; 5 in 006. Regions 001..006 across 2 rows → flow spans ~half the composition width (~3600px) and both rows (~2900px height). Fit: min(1544×0.76/3600, 633×0.76/2900) = min(0.326, 0.166) = 0.166 → clamped to minZoom 0.5 → but then it's NOT a fit — React Flow fitView with minZoom clamping: does it still center on the nodes? It sets zoom to minZoom and centers... hmm, apparently centered on map center or node bbox center? The flow object visible is at LEFT edge (x=344) — suggests the camera centered somewhere else (map center at zoom 0.5).

Actually wait — 0.166 < 0.16 (kernel minZoom 0.16!) — the FLOW's vertical span (2900px) can't even fit at kernel minZoom. So fitView clamps to minZoom=0.5 (my fit option) and... React Flow's fitView centers the bounding box of provided nodes even when clamped (it computes translate to center nodes at clamped zoom). The one flow object at x=344,y=286 (top-left-ish)... 8 flow objects; only 1 mounted means others are outside viewport. If it centered the bbox, more would be visible. Unless the corridor dock shift (-70px y) AFTER fit moved things slightly — only 70px, not enough to push out 7 objects.

Hmm wait — actually! Look again at `select()`:

```ts
window.setTimeout(() => {
  const instance = instanceRef.current;
  const targetNodes = instance?.getNodes().filter((node) => flowObjectIds.has(node.id)) ?? [];
  if (targetNodes.length > 0) void instance?.fitView({...});
}, 80);
```
`instance.getNodes()` returns ALL nodes incl. region nodes; filter by flow object ids → 8 object nodes ✓. fitView with nodes option ✓. Should center them.

BUT: fitView is called BEFORE the projection updates? No — setInteraction already applied (80ms later, projection re-rendered). Nodes exist. Hmm.

But wait — WHY only 10 object nodes mounted at zoom 0.5 with viewport 1544×633? At 0.5, visible graph area = 3088×1266. Objects are 232-296px wide → ~10 columns × 8 rows... should mount MANY (40+). onlyRenderVisibleElements renders nodes intersecting viewport + some buffer. 10 mounted total?? That's weirdly few. Unless... the mounted list query `.react-flow__node-object` — at 0.5 zoom... hmm 10 mounted, 1 flow object. 

OH WAIT. I bet I know: **onlyRenderVisibleElements uses the node's measured DOM position**, and after the row-composition change... no. Let me think differently: is the camera actually where fitView put it? translate(-1163.58, -1593.25) scale(0.5). Visible graph rect: x ∈ [2327, 5415], y ∈ [3186, 4452]. The complex map is 7295×5903. So we're looking at the middle-bottom-right region of the map. The 1 mounted flow object 005 at screen (344,286): graph pos = (344+1163)/0.5 = 3015, (286+1593)/0.5 = 3758. Object 5 in region 006.

Where's the flow bbox center? Objects spread across the whole composition... center ≈ (3600, 2900). Our camera center = (2327+5415)/2=3871, (3186+4452)/2=3819 — roughly right-bottom of center. Plausibly the flow bbox center is around there.

So the fit DID clamp to 0.5 and center the flow bbox — but at 0.5 the flow (spanning ~3600×2900+) needs zoom 0.166 to fully fit; at 0.5 only the central chunk is visible → 1 flow object + neighbors. The "10 mounted" is the count of intersecting nodes — at 0.5 zoom with nodes 116px wide... in a 3088×1266 window you'd expect ~15-25 nodes. 10 mounted — fine-ish (buffer margins).

So the REAL issue: **fixture Flow 1 spans the entire 2D composition** (both rows, all regions), because fixture flows thread objects round-robin across regions. NO zoom ≥0.5 can show the whole path. This is fixture construction, not a bug in my fit. For REAL flows (a bounded learning slice 5-12 nodes), they'd cluster in 1-2 regions.

But for acceptance evidence I need "运行 Trace 能看到从入口到结果的有序主路径". Options:

A. Use a different flow for the screenshot: complex has 3 flows: flow 1: start 0 len 8; flow 2: start 12, len 7 (objects 12-18); flow 3: start 24, len 7 (objects 24-30). Same round-robin spreading — all span regions.

B. Improve behavior: when the flow's fit zoom < minZoom, the corridor is carried by the strip (full order visible there) + canvas shows the local segment. That's defensible: "Trace 是稳定地图上的透镜" — lens shows local; corridor shows order. But acceptance says "看到从入口到结果的有序主路径"...

C. Camera follow: Trace could add step navigation — clicking a corridor step could pan to that object (keeps zoom). That makes the whole path walkable in order without zooming out: 有序主路径可沿走廊遍历。Nice but more work.

D. For the corridor fit, allow zoom below 0.5 when needed but ≥ kernel min 0.16: at 0.166 the map at landscape hides low-importance objects; flow objects (primary) remain; labels compensated. The full path visible with numbered edges — actually quite readable since only important+flow objects are rendered! Let me compute: fit zoom 0.166 → landscape level (threshold 0.5). visibleObjectIds at landscape in trace: important || primary || related → flow objects primary (8) + important objects (critical/high = indexes 0-7 of fixture... importance: index<2 critical, <8 high → objects 0..7 = the flow itself! 😄 plus others by search none). So at landscape+trace, visible = 8 flow objects + maybe a few more (indexes 0-7 are exactly flow 1 objects for complex!). The view: 8 objects spread across 7295×5903 at 0.166 → whole map visible, objects 232px×0.166 = 39px wide on screen — labels compensated ×4.5 max (13.5/(15×0.166) = 5.4 → clamped 4.5 → 15×0.166×4.5 = 11.2px — below floor!). Hmm 4.5 clamp → 11.2px. Raise max clamp? Label scale 5.4 on a 39px-wide node = text WAY overflows node. Landscape miniatures again.

So zooming way out is not the answer. For this pathological fixture, the corridor strip + local lens is the honest design. BUT I can make it much better with **corridor step click → camera pans to the step object** (keeps zoom at 0.5+ readable). Actually clicking a corridor step ALREADY selects the object (mode stays trace, selection changes — no camera move currently). Adding a camera pan on step click = "follow the corridor in order". Also the corridor auto-scrolls to keep current step in view. That delivers "有序主路径" traversal at readable density.

Also for realistic clustered flows, the fit works (0.5+).

Hmm, wait — but hold on: is fit-to-flow at 0.5 centered such that at least the corridor CENTER is readable? Yes.

Let me also reconsider: maybe lower flow-fit floor to 0.35 as compromise? At 0.35: architecture... no — 0.35 < 0.5 = landscape → normal/low objects hidden (fixture: only important+flow visible = 8-15 objects). Whole map at 0.35: 7295×0.35 = 2553 > 1544 width — still not fully visible vertically 5903×0.35 = 2066 > 633. Nope, doesn't fit either. 0.166 needed. So no zoom in [0.5,1.08] shows everything; and below 0.5 doesn't either until 0.16-0.2 (with 11px labels = unreadable). Conclusion: corridor strip + follow navigation is the right design. 

Implement:
1. TraceCorridor step click → onSelect (already selects object). In architecture-canvas, when selection changes during trace to an object, pan camera to center that object smoothly (keep zoom). Add to `select` callback: if kind === "object" and current.mode === "trace" → center camera on node. Center = node position + size/2 → setViewport({x: vw/2 - zoom*cx, ...}, duration 300). Need node rect from layout: layout.objectRects[id]. Also auto-scroll the rail to keep current chip visible (scrollIntoView({inline: "nearest"}) — do it in TraceCorridor via effect on selectedObjectId).

2. Also emphasize flow edges with sequence labels ✓ existing.

Also fix corridor rail overflow 290 — remaining overflow is OK with scroll + auto-scroll, but let me squeeze a bit more: reduce transition max-width 64, chip span max-width 120. 8 chips ≈ 8×(21+7+120+16padding+2border) ≈ 8×166 = 1328 + 7×(64+14+arrow) ≈ 7×85 = 595 → 1923?? still > 1544. Hmm the em (role) line adds width? em is inside span (block grid) so width = span max 128. Chip total ≈ 21+7+128+16+2 = 174. ×8 = 1392 + transitions 7×(64+14) = 546 → 1938. To fit 1500: transitions need → ~15px each?? Not feasible with payload text. 

